# `for (let i = 0; i <= b.length; i++) row.push(i);` with `b` proven undefined|string: the head reads b.length, so behind the loop b is a text. The loop is built twice: one copy proves it by the test in front, the other by the read in its head: two names of one value: at the join behind the loop both fell out of the state. They meet as a parameter now: the loops behind it know a text: the room of the list that the inner loop fills is asked for in front.
